BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Media School
DTSTART:20260918T140000Z
DTEND:20260918T154500Z
LOCATION:FRANKLIN HALL
GEO:39.167025;-86.526406
SUMMARY:Beyond the Arena: From Signage to Signal
DESCRIPTION:For a century\, a team's commercial ceiling was its DMA. Spon
 sorship meant physical assets — signage\, patches\, PA reads — sold t
 o reach the fans who walked through the door\, and only about 15% of a fa
 nbase ever attends a game in their lifetime.  In March 2026\, Pacers Spor
 ts &amp\; Entertainment launched Fieldhouse Media Network\, the first tea
 m-operated media network in sports\, built with Deloitte and Yieldmo. It 
 activates first-party fan data omnichannel — premium publishers\, apps\
 , CTV and social — across all 50 states and 57 countries\, turning fand
 om itself into a measurable media product.  The people who built it expla
 in what broke\, what they built\, and what it means for students entering
  media now.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  For a century\, a team's commercial cei
 ling was its DMA. Sponsorship meant physical assets — signage\, patches
 \, PA reads — sold to reach the fans who walked through the door\, and 
 only about 15% of a fanbase ever attends a game in their lifetime.</p><p>
   In March 2026\, Pacers Sports &amp\; Entertainment launched Fieldhouse 
 Media Network\, the first team-operated media network in sports\, built w
 ith Deloitte and Yieldmo. It activates first-party fan data omnichannel �
 �� premium publishers\, apps\, CTV and social — across all 50 states an
 d 57 countries\, turning fandom itself into a measurable media product.</
 p><p>  The people who built it explain what broke\, what they built\, and
  what it means for students entering media now.</p>
UID:20260918T140000Z-2412122@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260909T085744Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/mediaiub/event/2412122-beyond-the-arena-from-si
 gnage-to-signal
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
LAST-MODIFIED:20260909T125857Z
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2412122
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Join us as we explore how teams become media c
 ompanies and why there are no large or small markets anymore. Featuring <
 span style="font-size: 16px\;">Yieldmo\, Deloitte\, and Pacers Sports &am
 p\; Entertainment.</span>\n</p>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Media School
DTSTART:20260918T140000Z
DTEND:20260918T163000Z
LOCATION:SIDNEY AND LOIS ESKENAZI MUSEUM OF ART
GEO:39.169144;-86.519739
SUMMARY:Happiness 2.0 mask workshop
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the fall semester\, Indiana University undergradua
 te and high school students from the Bloomington community are invited to
  participate in the Happiness 2.0 Mask Workshops — an interactive exper
 ience that brings together science\, art\, and reflection on digital life
 .  The experience combines three components:an accessible discussion of c
 urrent research on digital media and identity  a guided exploration of ar
 tworks in the Eskenazi Museum's galleries  a hands-on mask-making activit
 y    Drawing inspiration from both the research discussion and the museum
  experience\, participants will create a mask representing aspects of the
 ir own digital identity and experiences with digital media. Topics explor
 ed throughout the workshop include identity formation\, online self-prese
 ntation\, the relationship between online and offline selves\, and well-b
 eing.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">  Throughout the f
 all semester\, Indiana University undergraduate and high school students 
 from the Bloomington community are invited to participate in the Happines
 s 2.0 Mask Workshops — an interactive experience that brings together s
 cience\, art\, and reflection on digital life.</p><p>  The experience com
 bines three components:</p><ul><li>an accessible discussion of current re
 search on digital media and identity  </li><li>a guided exploration of ar
 tworks in the Eskenazi Museum's galleries  </li><li>a hands-on mask-makin
 g activity  </li></ul><p>  Drawing inspiration from both the research dis
 cussion and the museum experience\, participants will create a mask repre
 senting aspects of their own digital identity and experiences with digita
 l media. Topics explored throughout the workshop include identity formati
 on\, online self-presentation\, the relationship between online and offli
 ne selves\, and well-being.</p>
UID:20260918T140000Z-2396817@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260826T105844Z
