BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Media School
DTSTART:20261023T173000Z
DTEND:20261023T181500Z
SUMMARY:Master Class in Documentary Filmmaking with Natalie Pattillo
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, October 231:30 - 2:15 PM  In-Person\, Lindley Hall 3
 28  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Month\, join award-winn
 ing documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a master class in document
 ary filmmaking.  This practical and interactive conversation will focus o
 n documentary filmmaking\, ethical storytelling\, working with vulnerable
  subjects\, survivor-centered narratives\, and the responsibilities of fi
 lmmakers telling stories involving trauma and violence.  —** You can al
 so join Natalie Pattillo on Friday\, October 23 at 2:30 PM for a Domestic
  Violence\, Storytelling\, and Social Change Workshop (https://events.iu.
 edu/kinsey/event/2383527-domestic-violence-storytelling-and-social-change
 ). Natalie's documentary film\, And So I Stayed will screen at the IU Cin
 ema on Saturday\, October 24 at 7:00 PM. **Natalie Pattillo is an award-w
 inning documentary filmmaker\, writer and journalist whose work explores 
 education\, child care access\, gender-based violence\, food and culture.
  Her work has appeared in The New York Times\, MSNBC\, VICE\, New York Ma
 gazine\, Al Jazeera America\, Salon and beyond. In 2020\, she received th
 e Media Award from the National Coalition Against Domestic Violence. She 
 was also named a Faculty News Champion by the Center for Community News a
 t the University of Vermont for her work supporting student journalism an
 d rebuilding local news.  Natalie was the co-director\, writer and produc
 er of And So I Stayed (https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?ur
 l=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F2021%2F06%2F11%2Fmovies%2Fdomestic-viol
 ence-law.html&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b2
 08def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379681
 366%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsI
 lAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=%
 2Fi1Tl7bk%2F8Acy%2B5RDgCzQCc%2F7gSL9gR5STvkaDCSvoQ%3D&amp\;reserved=0)\, 
 an award-winning documentary about survivors of domestic violence who wer
 e incarcerated for acts of survival. The film has been featured by The Ne
 w York Times\, the Chicago Tribune and ABC News.  She is currently workin
 g on Writing for My Mother (https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.co
 m/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.documentaries.org%2Ffilms%2Fwriting-for-my-mothe
 r%2F&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b208def48f0
 787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379713731%7CUnk
 nown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW
 4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=HJDUrVkh4J
 RuRMGml0LyUARQNxyWimVxZrLDyPnKf38%3D&amp\;reserved=0)\, a documentary and
  book project exploring literacy in the United States\, the communities t
 hat have been excluded from educational opportunities and what becomes po
 ssible in people's lives and for our democracy when those barriers are br
 oken down.  Natalie lives in Providence\, Rhode Island\, with her husband
  and three children. In her free time\, she can usually be found gardenin
 g\, knitting\, reading\, or taking unhurried walks.AND SO I STAYEDis an&#
 160\;eye-opening and award-winning documentary about three survivors of a
 buse fighting for their lives and how the legal system betrayed them.&#16
 0\;These women paid a steep price with long prison sentences\, lost time 
 with loved ones\, and painful memories. Formerly incarcerated survivor-ad
 vocate Kim Dadou Brown\, who met her wife while incarcerated\, is a drivi
 ng force in the passage of New York's Domestic Violence Survivors Justice
  Act (DVSJA)\, a new law meant to prevent survivors from receiving harsh 
 prison sentences for their acts of survival. Nikki Addimando\, a mother o
 f two young children\, suffered the consequences when a judge didn't foll
 ow the law's guidelines. Tanisha Davis\, a single mother who was ripped a
 way from her son in 2013\, is hopeful the new law is her way out of a har
 sh prison sentence.AND SO I STAYED aims to hold the legal system and publ
 ic accountable by exposing the unjust misogyny and racism embedded in the
 se power structures while also portraying the resilience\, hopes\, and dr
 eams of survivors and their loved ones.
