SUMMARY:Domestic Violence\, Storytelling\, and Social Change Workshop wit
 h Filmmaker Natalie Pattillo
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, October 232:30 - 3:30 PM  In-Person\, Lindley Hall 1
 02  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Month\, join award-winn
 ing documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a powerful discussion on d
 omestic violence.  The conversation will explore the criminalization of s
 urvivors\, the role of institutions and public policy\, and how communica
 tion and storytelling can raise awareness\, shape public opinion\, and dr
 ive social change.  —** You can also join Natalie Pattillo on Friday\, 
 October 23 at 1:30 PM for a Master Class in Documentary Filmmaking (https
 ://events.iu.edu/kinsey/event/2383524-master-class-in-documentary-filmmak
 ing-with).  Natalie's documentary film\, And So I Stayed will screen at t
 he IU Cinema on Saturday\, October 24 at 7:00 PM. **Natalie Pattillo is a
 n award-winning documentary filmmaker\, writer and journalist whose work 
 explores education\, child care access\, gender-based violence\, food and
  culture. Her work has appeared in The New York Times\, MSNBC\, VICE\, Ne
 w York Magazine\, Al Jazeera America\, Salon and beyond. In 2020\, she re
 ceived the Media Award from the National Coalition Against Domestic Viole
 nce. She was also named a Faculty News Champion by the Center for Communi
 ty News at the University of Vermont for her work supporting student jour
 nalism and rebuilding local news.  Natalie was the co-director\, writer a
 nd producer of And So I Stayed (https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outloo
 k.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F2021%2F06%2F11%2Fmovies%2Fdome
 stic-violence-law.html&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975
 c402135b208def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217
 092379681366%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAu
 MDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp
 \;sdata=%2Fi1Tl7bk%2F8Acy%2B5RDgCzQCc%2F7gSL9gR5STvkaDCSvoQ%3D&amp\;reser
 ved=0)\, an award-winning documentary about survivors of domestic violenc
 e who were incarcerated for acts of survival. The film has been featured 
 by The New York Times\, the Chicago Tribune and ABC News.  She is current
 ly working on Writing for My Mother (https://nam12.safelinks.protection.o
 utlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.documentaries.org%2Ffilms%2Fwriting-for
 -my-mother%2F&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b2
 08def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379713
 731%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsI
 lAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=H
 JDUrVkh4JRuRMGml0LyUARQNxyWimVxZrLDyPnKf38%3D&amp\;reserved=0)\, a docume
 ntary and book project exploring literacy in the United States\, the comm
 unities that have been excluded from educational opportunities and what b
 ecomes possible in people's lives and for our democracy when those barrie
 rs are broken down.  Natalie lives in Providence\, Rhode Island\, with he
 r husband and three children. In her free time\, she can usually be found
  gardening\, knitting\, reading\, or taking unhurried walks.AND SO I STAY
 EDis an eye-opening and award-winning documentary about three survivors o
 f abuse fighting for their lives and how the legal system betrayed them.T
 hese women paid a steep price with long prison sentences\, lost time with
  loved ones\, and painful memories. Formerly incarcerated survivor-advoca
 te Kim Dadou Brown\, who met her wife while incarcerated\, is a driving f
 orce in the passage of New York's Domestic Violence Survivors Justice Act
  (DVSJA)\, a new law meant to prevent survivors from receiving harsh pris
 on sentences for their acts of survival. Nikki Addimando\, a mother of tw
 o young children\, suffered the consequences when a judge didn't follow t
 he law's guidelines. Tanisha Davis\, a single mother who was ripped away 
 from her son in 2013\, is hopeful the new law is her way out of a harsh p
 rison sentence.AND SO I STAYED aims to hold the legal system and public a
 ccountable by exposing the unjust misogyny and racism embedded in these p
 ower structures while also portraying the resilience\, hopes\, and dreams
  of survivors and their loved ones.
