 Stayed_Hi-Res_Prouduction_Still_01.rev.1786929094.jpg 3x" data-max-w="320
 0" data-max-h="2200" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></picture>AND 
 SO I STAYED</strong></a></span><span class="s1">is an eye-opening and awa
 rd-winning documentary about three survivors of abuse fighting for their 
 lives and how the legal system betrayed them.</span></p><p class="p1"><sp
 an class="s1">These women paid a steep price with long prison sentences\,
  lost time with loved ones\, and painful memories. Formerly incarcerated 
 survivor-advocate Kim Dadou Brown\, who met her wife while incarcerated\,
  is a driving force in the passage of New York's Domestic Violence Surviv
 ors Justice Act (DVSJA)\, a new law meant to prevent survivors from recei
 ving harsh prison sentences for their acts of survival. Nikki Addimando\,
  a mother of two young children\, suffered the consequences when a judge 
 didn't follow the law's guidelines. Tanisha Davis\, a single mother who w
 as ripped away from her son in 2013\, is hopeful the new law is her way o
 ut of a harsh prison sentence.</span></p><p class="p1"><span class="s1"><
 span data-olk-copy-source="MailCompose">AND SO I STAYED aims to hold the 
 legal system and public accountable by exposing the unjust misogyny and r
 acism embedded in these power structures while also portraying the resili
 ence\, hopes\, and dreams of survivors and their loved ones.</span></span
 ></p>
UID:20261023T183000Z-2412127@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260909T092258Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2383527-domestic-violence-storytell
 ing-and-social-change
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
LAST-MODIFIED:20260909T132258Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/199/width/168/height/168/crop
 /1/src_region/0,0,3200,2133/32011_headshotNP_1.rev.1786914952.jpg
